WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.280
Identity and access management, or IAM, controls who can create, inspect, connect to,

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.000
modify, and delete cloud resources.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.965
Grant people and services only the permissions required for their role.

00:00:09.965 --> 00:00:13.516
Use separate accounts or projects for isolation where appropriate,

00:00:13.516 --> 00:00:16.977
require strong authentication for administrators, and review access regularly.

00:00:16.977 --> 00:00:21.240
A leaked credential with permission to launch GPU instances creates both security exposure

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.250
and immediate financial impact.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.000
Protect administrative access.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.420
Manage SSH keys centrally, remove stale keys,

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.000
and prefer short-lived or identity-backed access when available.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.965
Firewall rules should permit only necessary sources and ports.

00:00:33.965 --> 00:00:39.000
Do not expose notebook servers, dashboards, or model APIs publicly for convenience.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.150
Put services behind authenticated gateways, private networks, or secure tunnels,

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:47.000
and require API authentication and rate limits for shared inference.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.140
Store tokens, passwords,

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.550
and application keys in a secrets-management service rather than images, scripts,

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.000
notebooks, or shell history.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.870
Patch the operating system, drivers, containers, runtimes,

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:58.250
and web interfaces through a tested process.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:04.000
Monitor configuration changes, login activity, service errors, GPU and memory utilization,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.000
latency, cost, and unexpected network traffic.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Alerts should identify both degraded service and suspicious resource creation.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.110
Prepare incident actions before they are needed: isolate the instance, revoke credentials,

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.195
preserve appropriate logs, restore a trusted image, and assess exposed data.

00:01:18.195 --> 00:01:22.850
Delete or stop unused compute, detach public addresses, and remove abandoned disks,

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:26.000
snapshots, keys, and firewall rules according to retention requirements.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Operational hygiene closes the gap between a secure launch and a secure lifecycle.
